#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# gradient_color

#% text
# `gradient_color(g, t)` gives the colour at t, between 0 and 1, of the colour gradient g.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# g is the name of a gradient or a list of colours; the colours of a list are spread evenly from 0 to 1 and mixed in between.

#% item
# The named gradients: `viridis` (violet through blue and green to yellow, the default of density and contour plots), `thermal` (black through red and orange to pale yellow), `cool_warm` (blue through gray to red), `ocean`, `earth`, `rainbow` and `gray`.

#% item
# t below 0 counts as 0 and above 1 as 1. A t that is not a number yet leaves the call as it is.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The middle of the gray gradient:

#% cell
gradient_color(gray, 0.5)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.5, 0.5, 0.5)

#% text
# A gradient through two colours:

#% cell
gradient_color([red, blue], 1/4)
#% output
#= rgb_color(0.75, 0.0, 0.25)

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Disks coloured along the default gradient:

#% cell
graphics(table([gradient_color(viridis, k/8), disk([k, 0], 0.45)], k, range(0, 8)))
#% output
#= image("gradient_color.files/gradient_color-3.png")

#% text
# The named gradients side by side:

#% cell
gs = [viridis, thermal, cool_warm, ocean, earth, rainbow, gray]; graphics(table(raster([table(k/40, k, range(0, 40))], [[0, -2j], [8, 1 - 2j]], gs[j]), j, 7))
#% output
#= image("gradient_color.files/gradient_color-4.png")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `density_plot`, `contour_plot`, `raster`, `blend`
